{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The footsteps were slow and even, each one ringing on the iron stair like a bell struck underwater. Mira counted them without meaning to. Twenty. Forty. The spiral held one hundred and twelve steps; she had climbed them every night for three years and knew the number the way she knew her own pulse. Tobias let go of her hand and crossed to the desk. He bent over the logbook, and she watched the color leave his face. \"What does it say?\" she asked. He didn't answer at first. Then he read it aloud, his voice thin: \"'Keeper returned. Light maintained. All well.'\" Below them, the footsteps paused at the landing where the old storeroom was, the one they never used, the one with the rusted padlock and the smell of lamp oil that never quite faded. Mira remembered the photograph she had found there in her first week, a man in a heavy coat standing at the gallery rail, squinting into the sun. On the back someone had written a name and a date, and beneath it, in smaller letters: lost in the storm. The steps began again. Sixty. Eighty. The lens turned above them, patient and indifferent, throwing its white arm across the sea. Tobias closed the logbook, as though that might undo the words inside it. Mira found she was no longer afraid, exactly. What she felt was closer to recognition. At one hundred and eleven, the footsteps stopped. There was a soft knock at the lantern room door, courteous, almost apologetic. \"It's his watch,\" Mira said quietly. \"He's come to relieve us.\""}
